Reference: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=XLC1OebUdns&t=342s

2️⃣ How HAR Helps

HAR files capture actual HTTP traffic (requests and responses) from a real session with the payment gateway.
1.You perform one manual transaction or test run.
2.Playwright records all requests and responses to the payment APIs in a HAR file.
3.Later, your automated tests can replay the HAR, so:
..The frontend thinks it’s talking to the real payment API.
..The backend receives mocked responses from HAR.
No real money is charged, and tests are fully reproducible.

Example Scenario
Real Flow:
User enters card details and clicks "Pay".
Frontend sends POST /payments with card info to Payment Gateway.
Gateway responds with:
{
"status": "success",
"transactionId": "TX12345"
}
Frontend shows "Payment Successful".
Problem in automation: Hitting live API is risky.

---

HAR-based Mocking Flow:
Record HAR while performing a manual payment:
{
"request": {
"url": "https://payment-gateway.com/api/payments",
"method": "POST",
"body": { "cardNumber": "4111...", "amount": 100 }
},
"response": {
"status": 200,
"body": { "status": "success", "transactionId": "TX12345" }
}
}
In Playwright, replay the HAR in your automation:
await context.routeFromHAR('payment.har');

---

When the automation clicks "Pay":
The request is intercepted.
HAR returns the recorded response immediately.
Frontend shows "Payment Successful" without touching the real API.
